	abstract = {Software-Defined Networking (SDN) integrated with fog-enabled internet of things environments improves scalability, flexibility, and centralized network management. However, the presence of heterogeneous IoT devices and resource-constrained fog nodes significantly increases vulnerability to large-scale Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS) attacks. Existing DDoS detection solutions often rely on centralized learning or computationally intensive deep learning models, which leads to higher latency and reduced real-time applicability. To address these issues, this research proposes a Federated Multi-Phase Lightweight Deep Learning (FedMP-LDL) framework for efficient DDoS detection and mitigation in SDN–fog–IoT networks. The novelty of FedMP-LDL is derived from its multi-phase architecture integrating Hamacher-based fuzzy equivalence rules for lightweight anomaly detection and a progressive distilled ghost-squeeze excitation network for hierarchical federated learning across fog nodes. In addition, multi-objective reinforcement learning is incorporated to enable adaptive SDN flow-rule optimization and dynamic DDoS mitigation in fog-enabled IoT environments. This distributed strategy eliminates dependency on centralized training, enhances scalability, and maintains robust performance under varying attack intensities. Experimental results demonstrate that FedMP-LDL achieves 99.72% accuracy, 99.66% precision, 99.57% recall, and 99.61% F1-score with a low inference time of 0.1762 s, outperforming existing state-of-the-art approaches while ensuring an effective balance between detection accuracy and computational efficiency. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
