	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105051724789?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Flooding attacks against IoT-enabled fog nodes in renewable-integrated smart grids threaten the telemetry that load frequency control (LFC) and automatic voltage control (AVC) depend on, risking frequency and voltage instability and disrupted dispatch. This paper presents a blockchain-secured machine learning framework for real-time intrusion detection in fog-based IoT environments. A four-layer architecture combines IoT edge monitoring, fog-side extraction of 32 engineered traffic features, a multi-model detection engine, and a blockchain layer with three smart contracts for automated response, tamper-proof logging, and control validation. The framework is evaluated on the SmartGridDDoS dataset (57,779 traffic windows) under normal conditions and three flooding attack types: UDP, TCP, and ICMP. Four classifiers (RF, GBM, MLP, SVM) achieve above 99.6% accuracy, above 97.9% F1-score, and 99.94% ROC-AUC, with TCP and ICMP flooding detected at 100% F1 and UDP at 93.88% F1; five-fold cross-validation yields 98.059 ± 0.212% mean F1. Detection completes within 107 ms, within the IEC 61850 real-time control budget and typical LFC/AVC sampling intervals; direct measurement of the resulting frequency deviation and voltage regulation error falls outside the scope of this dataset-level evaluation. Restricting detection to volumetric features alone reduces F1–75.89%, confirming the value of the full feature set; the blockchain layer adds tamper-evident logging and automated threat response without a single point of failure. These results show that fog-side machine learning detection combined with blockchain-secured response can preserve the data integrity underlying LFC and AVC automation during active flooding attacks. © 2026 Elsevier Inc.},
