	abstract = {Organizational cybersecurity systems use AI technology to detect threats actively through their ability to monitor systems in real-time continuously. AI-driven systems achieve high performance in anomaly detection and malware classification and intrusion prevention through their ability to process large datasets and their adaptive learning models. The growing expertise of attackers has led to the development of advanced methods which attackers use to conduct adversarial machine learning (AML) attacks. The attackers use these methods to add tiny unnoticeable changes to data entries which they use to trick AI systems. The implementation of these methods creates a major obstacle which affects the dependability and operational stability and trustworthiness of artificial intelligence-based security systems. The research paper provides a detailed analysis of three main adversarial attack methods which include evasion attacks and poisoning attacks and model inversion attacks while demonstrating their impact on cybersecurity systems. The research evaluates five established defense systems which consist of adversarial training and input preprocessing and feature squeezing and model distillation and robust optimization. The research presents an original method which unites adversarial resistance with explainable AI (XAI) systems. The system combines various elements to form a threat detection system which provides both precise and reliable security monitoring capabilities. The framework receives empirical verification through testing with CIC-IDS2017 and TON_IoT which are recognized standard benchmark datasets. The testing procedure includes three attack scenarios which consist of Fast Gradient Sign Method (FGSM) and Projected Gradient Descent (PGD) and Carlini & Wagner (C&W) attacks. The experimental results show that the hybrid system achieves superior attack detection precision and simultaneously offers improved understanding for required forensic analysis. Th