	abstract = {The rapid growth of the Internet of Things (IoT) devices has significantly expanded the attack surface of networks. In contrast, the resource constraints of IoT devices hinder the use of traditional cryptographic methods or continuous patching. To address these challenges, an adaptive cyber defence framework is proposed that combines lightweight authentication with real-time Anomaly detection and automated responses. This framework involves three main parties: the IoT device, the Gateway server, and a centralised Threat-Control Service(TCS). All parties share long- term symmetric keys and participate in a five-step nonce-based challenge-response handshake over an unsecured network. The session key is derived using hash functions, XOR, concatenation, and symmetric encryption techniques. Additionally, TCS collects handshake metadata, such as timing, nonce reuse, and integrity- check failures, to detect anomalies through a one-class machine- learning classifier. When an anomaly is detected, TCS can revoke tokens, devices or initiate firmware updates. Experimental results show that the framework achieves an average handshake latency of 180 ms, with 95% accuracy in anomaly detection, enhancing IoT security with a dynamic, context-aware approach. © 2025 IEEE.},
