	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034897853?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Anomaly detection is a fundamental aspect of securing expansive internet of Things (IoT) networks, which comprise billions of interconnected devices vulnerable to a wide spectrum of cyber threats. Effective anomaly detection is vital as it enables the early identification of malicious activities, thereby reducing the likelihood of service interruptions, information breaches, and potential safety risks. Traditional methods struggle to adapt to emerging threats and manage the complexity of large-scale IoT traffic. To address these limitations, this study proposes a hybrid deep learning (DL) model integrating a One-Dimensional Convolutional Neural Network (1D-CNN) with a Transformer Encoder for robust anomaly classification in IoT networks. The model processes traffic data from the CIC-IoT 2023 dataset, which includes benign traffic and seven major attack categories such as DDoS, DoS, Recon, Web, Brute Force, Spoofing, and Mirai. The 1D-CNN component extracts local temporal patterns and short-range dependencies crucial for detecting subtle anomalies, while the Transformer Encoder captures global context and long-range feature dependencies for improved classification accuracy. Performance evaluation using standard metrics demonstrates the model's effectiveness, achieving an accuracy of 95.82%, precision of 95.20%, recall of 94.80%, F1-score of 93.95%, and a low misclassification score of 4.18%. These results confirm that the proposed hybrid architecture outperforms traditional methods in handling complex, large-scale IoT traffic, offering a scalable and adaptive solution for real-world anomaly detection and intrusion prevention systems. © 2025 IEEE.},
