	abstract = {The escalating number of Internet of Things (IoT) devices is leading to serious cybersecurity issues because these devices are limited in their computing power and susceptible to different network attacks. Most traditional intrusion detection systems are based on centralized structures and heavy computation models; thus, they cannot be easily used for real-time applications in environments with limited resources. Here we introduce GuardianPi, an IoT security system that is based on the edge and can operate efficiently and in real time. The system uses a Raspberry Pi as a monitoring node at the edge to record network traffic with the help of a lightweight packet inspecting technique. The recorded traffic is converted into flow-based features by CICFlowMeter, which allows for more structured and efficient data representation for machine learning. Our model of Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (BiLSTM) is employed to examine the patterns of sequence traffic and to classify network flows accurately as either benign or malicious. A DistilBERT-based module, to make the system more interpretable, gives short and human-readable explanations for the network threats detected. Also, a robotic intrusion prevention method, in real time, updates firewall rules to exclude the malicious sources automatically.Our system achieves a classification accuracy of 99.48%, with its precision, recall, and F1-scores above 0.99 across all attack categories. Our model shows stability in its performance in both training and validation, and hyperparameter tuning shows minimal impact on overall performance metrics. Additionally, the label shuffling validation showed a near-random performance, through which we can infer that the model learns meaningful patterns instead of memorizing the data. © 2026 IEEE.},
