	abstract = {'Security' is a primary concern for Internet of Things (IoT) devices due to their diverse applications. Potential attacks in these applications are constantly emerging and evolving. Moreover, it creates a need to update the existing Machine Learning (ML)- based Intrusion Detection System (IDS) to address these challenges. Of late, many lightweight protocols have been proposed for communication in these devices. Among them, Message Queuing Telemetry Transport (MQTT) has gained popularity for its low memory consumption and minimal bandwidth requirements. However, this protocol lacks built-in encryption, authentication, and access control mechanisms, which pose a threat to secure communications. This research presents an efficient IDS framework integrating metaheuristic feature selection, i.e. Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO), eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGB), and Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LGBM), known as advanced ensemble learners to classify benign and attack scenarios in MQTT-based IoT environments. PSO overcomes local optima traps in feature selection, identifying the most critical parameters for traffic classification. In this approach, optimized features have been processed using a dual-ensemble classifier like XGB, selected for its rigorous L1/L2 regularization that creates an ability to handle network-induced data sparsity. Whereas, LGBM aids in its leaf-wise growth strategy and high-speed execution at the IoT edge. Experimental results on the MQTT-IoT-IDS2020 dataset indicate that the proposed XGB-PSO model have achieved an exceptional precision of 99.98% and accuracy of 90.14%, while the LGBM-PSO model have achieved a precision of 99.31% and accuracy of 90.15%. To validate the generalizability of this framework against modern threats, these models being tested on an alternative benchmark dataset DoS/DDoS-MQTT-IoT. Validation attributes that the XGB-PSO achieves commendable accuracy (91.23%)and precision (96.95%), while the LGBM-PSO model meets 89.62% and 96.03%, respectively. Interpretability and robustness of the model are ensured through SHapley Additive Explanations (SHAP)-based feature attribution and Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique (SMOTE)-driven imbalance mitigation, to validate the performance of the proposed framework. These findings clearly show that combining metaheuristic feature refinement with an ensemble approach could be an exciting choice for authoritative and adaptable solutions in modern IoT attack vectors. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2026.},
	author_keywords = {eXtreme gradient boosting; Internet of things; Intrusion detection system; Light gradient boosting machine; Machine learning; Message queuing telemetry transport; Particle swarm optimization; SHapley additive explanations; Synthetic minority oversampling technique},
