	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/ISADES69945.2026.11608173},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046066741?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The high rate of incorporation of renewable energy sources into smart grid systems has brought about sensitive cybersecurity weaknesses that jeopardize the stability, reliability, and security of contemporary power systems. Smart grids are being targeted by advanced cyberattacks such as denial-of-service (DoS), man-in-themiddle (MITM), false data injection (FDI), reconnaissance, and replay attacks as they become increasingly reliant on networks of interconnected communication, supervisory control and data acquisition (SCADA) systems, and Internet of Things (IoT) devices. This paper is a detailed cybersecurity architecture of the smart grid renewable energy systems that incorporates sophisticated detection, analysis, and automated mitigation. The suggested framework incorporates an autoencoder-based anomaly detection algorithm with an ensemble machine learning classifier based on a weighted fusion strategy that incorporates the use of both algorithms (Random Forest and Gradient Boosting). New adaptive hybrid information gain and autoencoder-based feature selection algorithms are proposed to maximize the input feature space thus enhancing the detection accuracy but minimizing the computational cost. The framework has an overall detection accuracy of 98.4 and precision, recall and F1score exceeding 97 percent among all types of attacks. The experimental testing shows that the suggested method is superior to state-of-the-art methods. Moreover, the framework has the built-in real-time automated mitigation module, which can respond to identified threats within The time required ranges from 25 milliseconds (low-load conditions) to 125 milliseconds (peak DDoS storm conditions), with a median time of about 48 milliseconds across all test conditions.  © 2026 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Cybersecurity; Intrusion Detection; Machine Learning; Renewable Energy; Smart Grid},
	keywords = {Anomaly detection; Cybersecurity; Data communication systems; Electric power transmission networks; Energy policy; Feature extraction; Internet of things; Intrusion detection; Learning algorithms; Natural resources; Network security; Renewable energy; Smart power grids; Auto encoders; Cyber security; Detection accuracy; Energy systems; High rate; Intrusion-Detection; Machine-learning; Renewable energies; Renewable energy source; Smart grid; Learning systems},
