	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045167017?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Bird intrusion into agricultural farmlands results in significant crop losses, especially during the crucial growth and harvest stages. The traditional methods of bird deterrence, such as scarecrows, static sound emitters, and manual surveillance, tend to become ineffective as birds become habituated and lack adaptability. To address these challenges, an intelligent bird intrusion detection and frequency, adaptive repelling system is proposed that aims at automatically providing an efficient and environmentally friendly solution for crop protection in the fields. The system uses image recognition, based on deep learning algorithms, to detect and recognise birds in real time from visual data obtained through cameras in the fields. Once a bird is detected, the system generates on, demand species, specific acoustic frequencies and emits them locally through a sound emission module enabled by the Internet of Things (IoT). In contrast to conventional repellers that depend on fixed sound patterns, the adaptive frequency approach changes the acoustic signals according to the birds' response behaviour, thus reducing habituation and increasing the deterrence effectiveness over time. In fact, the whole system functions autonomously with very little human involvement, and it is amenable to being set up in villages. Field tests reveal that employing adaptive frequency modulation results in a more significant improvement of bird repelling effectiveness over static mitigation measures, while at the same time allowing an ethical, harm-free method. This system is a step forward in the development of smart agricultural guarding, which is achieved by the combination of artificial intelligence, adaptive acoustics, and IoT.  © 2026 IEEE.},
