	pages = {1877 - 1900},
	doi = {10.3837/tiis.2026.04.014},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037528747?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The increasing reliance on IoT devices in critical infrastructures has amplified their exposure to cyber threats, necessitating intrusion detection systems (IDS) that not only ensure high detection accuracy but also minimize latency and false alarms. This paper proposes a metaheuristic-optimized deep learning framework that overcomes key limitations of traditional IDS: high false alarm rates, computational complexity, and lack of interpretability. Leveraging the UNSW-NB15 dataset, the framework incorporates BEGAN-based oversampling and Tomek Links undersampling for balanced data preparation, Fast Correlation-Based Feature Selection (FCBF) for reducing feature redundancy, and a Tab Transformer model tuned via the Seagull Optimization Algorithm (SOA) to enhance classification performance. A KNN-based post-processing module further refines predictions by correcting potential misclassifications, significantly reducing false positives and avoiding unnecessary alerts. To support real-time deployment in latency-sensitive IoT environments, the system achieves an inference latency of just 0.0139 ms. Additionally, SHAP and LIME are employed for local interpretability, while a Gemini large language model assists in generating actionable mitigation strategies. Experimental results confirm the framework's robustness, achieving 99.24% accuracy, a 0.2% false positive rate, and low latency, making it highly effective and interpretable for secure IoT monitoring applications. © 2026 KSII.},
