	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/ISPACS68724.2025.11382588},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034745741?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Several state-of-the-art (SoTA) studies have shown that machine learning models such as random forest are optimal for intrusion detection systems using the CICIDS2017 benchmark dataset. However, resource-constrained environments such as the Internet of Things (IoT) limit model complexity, where the model needs to be evaluated for efficiency. Our research aims to apply and assess a hybrid pruning and quantization method, Prune-Q, on IDS in resource-constrained environments, such as IoT. We used the benchmark dataset CICIDS2017, which contains 14 types of attacks. We used several preprocessing stages, such as imbalanced dataset mitigation using a hybrid sampling method: multi-label random undersampling-synthetic minority oversampling technique (ML-RUSMOTE). We then built a deep neural network (DNN) model and compressed it using pruning and quantization to produce Prune-Q. We compared its performance with random forest, which is a SoTA method for IDS using CICIDS2017. We used accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score for model performance, and CR for model efficiency. The test results show that the optimum target count T for ML-RUSMOTE is 4000, which provides the highest recall, which is 0.87. The recall result is the highest compared to the RUS+ADASYN, RUS+class weights, and RUS sampling methods. Then, although the performance of our Prune-Q DNN model is below the random forest model, the size of our model is 513.5 × smaller, which is 3.8 KB compared to 4,416.6 KB. This study concludes that our proposed Prune-Q DNN is the most efficient for IDSs in resource-constrained environments using the CICIDS2017 dataset. © 2025 IEEE.},
